<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Moves a 1.x installation's data into the sandbox container on the first
  sandboxed launch, and only then.

  ## What this is for

  Until 2.0 CoreTend was not sandboxed, so its database lived at
  `~/Library/Application Support/CoreTend` — outside any container. A
  sandboxed build reads `Application Support` inside its own container, which
  on a machine upgrading from 1.0.2 is empty. Without this file the upgrade
  looks like a factory reset: the Record empty, every exclusion gone, every
  favourite gone. The data would still be on disk, unreachable, which is worse
  than deleted because nothing would say so.

  macOS applies this once, at the first launch of the sandboxed build, before
  any of the app's own code runs. It is not a fallback the app can call.

  ## What moves, and what deliberately does not

  `Application Support/CoreTend` holds the SQLite store: the activity table,
  the append-only `safety_log`, exclusions, favourites and locations. All of
  it is the user's own record of what CoreTend did on their Mac, and losing it
  would break the one promise this product is built on.

  **Locations and exclusions move, but they do not keep working by moving.**
  They are stored as plain paths, and a plain path is not access. Under the
  sandbox each one has to be re-granted through an open panel before CoreTend
  may read it again. The rows are kept so the app can say *which* folders need
  re-granting instead of silently forgetting them — see
  `docs/APP_STORE_TRANSITION.md`, phase 4.

  The preference file moves too (added 2026-09-23). This said moving the
  preference domain was `LegacyDataMigration`'s job, in code — but inside a
  container that code cannot read ~/Library/Preferences at all, and
  `UserDefaults` reads the container's own copy. Without this line an upgrade
  kept the Record and lost every setting: the menu bar item, the language,
  the sidebar arrangement, the scheduled scan, onboarding done. Caches are
  regenerable and stay behind.

  Consequence for coexistence: this is a move, not a copy. A 1.0.2 copy left
  installed and launched afterwards finds an empty store and default
  settings. docs/migration/upgrade-validation.md covers it.

  ## Verification

  This cannot be tested on an unsandboxed build: nothing applies it. It is
  verified on a Mac that has 1.0.2 installed, by installing the sandboxed
  build over it and checking that the Record still lists the same operations.
  Until that has been done on real hardware it stays unverified, and
  `docs/APP_STORE_TRANSITION.md` says so rather than assuming.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Move</key>
	<array>
		<string>${HOME}/Library/Application Support/CoreTend</string>
		<string>${HOME}/Library/Preferences/com.ahmetbsbnr.coretend.plist</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
